"Superfluid Helium: teaching notes",
"Superfluid Helium",
"Intro thermodynamics or modern physics",
"Liquid helium {#} has a phase transition at {#} K, the lambda point. Below it, helium II behaves as two interpenetrating fluids: a normal fluid that carries all the entropy and has viscosity, and a superfluid with neither.",
"Evaporation cools a liquid: each mole that leaves takes its latent heat. Pumping on helium cools it to the lambda point and below, and the boiling stops at once because helium II conducts heat so well that no bubbles can form.",
"The superfluid flows where the normal fluid cannot: through fine powder (the fountain effect), up walls as a film, and it stays still between turning disks.",
"Students should know latent heat, vapor pressure and entropy. The demo has five experiments, every number from measured helium properties (Donnelly and Barenghi {#}): <strong>Pump down</strong> (a dewar of {#} g of helium at {#} K under a pump), <strong>Fountain</strong> (a tube with a powder plug and a heater), <strong>Creeping film</strong> (a beaker hanging in a {#} K bath) <strong>Spinning disks</strong> (a stack of {#} aluminum disks on a torsion fiber, after Andronikashvili) and <strong>Helium {#}</strong> (a cell squeezed so part of it freezes). Speed buttons run time faster.",
"<strong>Pump down through the lambda point.</strong> Ask Q1. The temperature falls quickly at first, the bubbles stop at {#} K, and the graph pauses there because the heat capacity peaks. With a {#} L/s pump and a {#} mW heat leak it takes about {#} minutes, and about {#} % of the liquid boils away to get there (Q2).",
"<strong>A bigger pump.</strong> Four times the speed reaches the lambda point four times sooner and ends colder.",
"<strong>Fountain.</strong> At {#} K a {#} mK rise inside the tube holds a column {#} cm high; at {#} K and {#} mK it would hold {#} cm, so it spouts out of the {#} cm tube (Q3).",
"<strong>Film.</strong> The beaker empties itself at a steady rate (Q4): a straight line on the graph. Pushed down into the bath, an empty beaker fills itself.",
"<strong>Spinning disks.</strong> At {#} K the period is {#} s, almost the bare disks' {#} s. Record periods at several temperatures up to {#} K; the dots trace the normal fluid fraction (Q5).",
"<strong>Helium {#}: cooling by freezing.</strong> Ask Q6 first. A cell of helium {#} at {#} mK is squeezed; freezing cools it along the melting curve, and the pressure trace passes the A transition at {#} MPa ({#} mK) and the B transition at {#} MPa ({#} mK), the trace in which Osheroff, Richardson and Lee found superfluid helium {#} in {#}. With a bigger heat leak the cell stalls above the transition.",
"<strong>Q1.</strong> What happens to the bath's temperature and to the bubbles as the pump lowers the pressure? Predict what changes at {#} K.",
"<strong>Q2.</strong> The latent heat is about {#} J/mol and the heat capacity about {#} J/(mol·K) on average between {#} K and {#} K. Estimate the fraction of the liquid that boils away to cool the rest to the lambda point, with no heat leak.",
"<strong>Q3.</strong> The fountain pressure is ΔP = ρSΔT, so the column it holds is h = SΔT/g. At {#} K, S = {#} J/(kg·K). How high a column does a {#} mK rise hold?",
"<strong>Q4.</strong> The film carries {#} × {#}⁻⁵ cm³/s for each centimeter of rim. How fast does the level fall in a beaker {#} mm in inside radius, and how long does it take to drop {#} mm?",
"<strong>Q5.</strong> The helium between the disks has {#} times the bare disks' moment of inertia at {#} K ({#} % more at {#} K, where the liquid is denser). If only the normal fluid turns with the disks, find ρn/ρ at {#} K from the period {#} s (bare disks {#} s), and predict the period above the lambda point.",
"<strong>Q6.</strong> Liquid helium {#} near its melting pressure has entropy S = γT with γ = {#}R per kelvin; the solid's disordered nuclear spins have S = R ln {#}. What share of the liquid must freeze, with no heat leak, to cool the rest from {#} mK to the superfluid transition at {#} mK?",
"<strong>\"Superfluid helium is just very cold, very thin liquid.\"</strong> It is a different phase with a sharp transition and a heat capacity peak; the superfluid part has zero viscosity, not just a small one.",
"<strong>\"The boiling stops because the pump stops working.\"</strong> The pump keeps pumping and the liquid keeps evaporating, now only from the surface: helium II carries heat so well that no part of it gets warm enough to make a bubble.",
"<strong>\"The film climbs because helium defies gravity.\"</strong> All liquids that wet a wall form a film; in helium II the superfluid in the film flows without friction, so the film carries liquid over the rim to the lower level.",
"<strong>\"Freezing releases heat, so squeezing a liquid into a solid warms it.\"</strong> Below {#} K solid helium {#} has more entropy than the liquid (its nuclear spins are disordered while the liquid's are not), so freezing absorbs heat: the Pomeranchuk effect.",
"<strong>\"Below the lambda point all the liquid is superfluid.\"</strong> The superfluid fraction grows from zero at {#} K to about {#} % at {#} K.",
"Vapor pressure from the ITS-{#} helium equations (BIPM), matching Donnelly and Barenghi Table {#} within {#} %. Entropy, heat capacity, latent heat, density and ρn/ρ are their tabulated values, with monotone cubic interpolation between rows (in ln S for the entropy).",
"Pump down: the pump takes gas at room temperature, ṅ = SpP/(RT); the bath obeys nC dT/dt = Q − Lṅ, with a {#} mW heat leak. The bubbles are drawn to show boiling, not computed.",
"Fountain: H. London's relation dP = ρS dT integrated over the temperature rise, with ρ taken constant (it varies by under {#} % from {#} to {#} K).",
"Film: the Rollin film transfer rate of {#} × {#}⁻⁵ cm³/s per cm of the narrowest perimeter below {#} K (Daunt and Mendelssohn {#}), independent of the height difference; the film is drawn far thicker than its {#} nm.",
"Helium {#}: melting pressure from the PLTS-{#} equation (BIPM), which defines the A and A to B points on the melting curve; the superfluid boundary at lower pressure is Greywall's ({#}), converted to PLTS-{#}. Cooling by freezing uses entropy balance with S_solid = R ln {#} and S_liquid = γT; the liquid's drop in entropy below the superfluid transition and the solid's ordering near {#} mK are left out, so the model stops at {#} mK.",
"Spinning disks: the period is {#}π√((I_disks + (ρn/ρ)I_He)/κ), with the fiber chosen to give {#} s for the bare disks.",
"Pair with <strong>States of Matter</strong> (phase changes and latent heat) and <strong>Superconductors and the Meissner Effect</strong> (another quantum state with zero resistance to flow).",
